{"step_index":1771,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-12T00:04:11Z","thinking":"**Bounding Connected Graph Radii**\n\nThe statement asserts that connected graphs with seven or fewer vertices always have a radius of at most 3. The longest path possible within such graphs, P7, has a radius of 3. This aligns with the general upper bound of $\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor$ for a graph with $n$ vertices. The calculations confirm the upper bound with n = 7.\n\n\n**Evaluating Potential Counterexamples**\n\nIf the graph radius is at least 4, then the number of vertices must be at least 8.  Consider cases where W, the maximum distance, is less than the number of vertices.  W = 4 cannot result in a graph radius of 4 or more, since any graph covered by two radius-1 balls will have radius at most 3. If W = 5 and the graph radius is 4, it is not a counterexample because radius + 1 ≤ W. To be a counterexample with W=5, the graph radius needs to be at least 5.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"tail -n 25 /tmp/agyhome.NWyWnj/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/f458c9cf-488d-4cd8-8365-7c4e4b16e3f9/.system_generated/logs/transcript.jsonl\"","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\"","RunPersistent":"false","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Check conductor transcript\"","toolSummary":"\"View conductor transcript tail\""}}]}
